\image(figures/permanova.svg)
// result_end

Management explains about a third of the variation in dune vegetation (R² = 0.34, p < 0.001 entered first), and the A1 horizon a further tenth. Their interaction is not significant. Because the terms are correlated (A1 differs between management types), the sequential partition depends on the order: entered first, A1 claims 0.17 rather than 0.10, and Management drops to 0.28. Each term's unique contribution, adjusted for the other terms, is much smaller. In the marginal table with land use added, neither Management (p = 0.058) nor A1 (p = 0.086) reaches 5%. Much of the explained variation is shared among correlated predictors, so the type of sums of squares has to match the question being asked.

The dispersion test changes the interpretation of the Management effect. The nature-conservation sites (NM) are far more variable (mean distance to the group center 0.45) than biologically farmed sites (BF, 0.22), as the ordination shows. Around centroids, the dispersions differ significantly (p = 0.029). Around spatial medians, which resist the outlying NM sites, they do not (p = 0.16). With groups of three to six sites, the two summaries disagree. The PERMANOVA result therefore reflects a shift in composition together with an uncertain difference in spread. The Bray–Curtis PCoA has five negative eigenvalues (6% of the positive inertia). Keeping the imaginary axes, as `dispersion_test` does, means the PCoA-based distances to the center reproduce the original dissimilarities, without first making the dissimilarity Euclidean.

The simulation reproduces the results of Anderson and Walsh (2013). With balanced groups, PERMANOVA is almost unaffected by a twofold difference in dispersion (4.8% rejections at the 5% level), while PERMDISP detects the difference every time. With unbalanced groups, unequal dispersion distorts PERMANOVA. When the smaller group is more dispersed, PERMANOVA rejects 61% of the time with identical centroids, so a significant result here does not establish a location difference. When the larger group is more dispersed, PERMANOVA never rejects, so a real location effect could be missed. PERMDISP holds its level under a pure location shift. Its only flaw shows with unbalanced equal-dispersion groups, where it rejects 8% of the time because distances to a small group's center are biased low. The bias adjustment of Stier et al. (2013) restores 5%.

>> Practical guidance

- Report PERMANOVA together with PERMDISP. A significant PERMANOVA with significant dispersion differences cannot be interpreted as a pure location effect, especially in unbalanced designs. Use balanced designs where possible.
- Choose sequential or marginal partitions deliberately, and state which was used. With correlated predictors they answer different questions.
- Use the bias-adjusted dispersion test with unequal group sizes, and compare centroid and spatial-median versions when groups are small or contain outliers.
- Restrict permutations to strata (blocks, plots, time series) when the design is hierarchical. Free permutation of non-exchangeable units gives invalid p-values.

>> References

- M. J. Anderson (2001). A new method for non-parametric multivariate analysis of variance. *Austral Ecology* 26(1), 32-46. doi:10.1111/j.1442-9993.2001.01070.pp.x
- B. H. McArdle and M. J. Anderson (2001). Fitting multivariate models to community data: a comment on distance-based redundancy analysis. *Ecology* 82(1), 290-297. doi:10.1890/0012-9658(2001)082[0290:FMMTCD]2.0.CO;2
- M. J. Anderson (2006). Distance-based tests for homogeneity of multivariate dispersions. *Biometrics* 62(1), 245-253. doi:10.1111/j.1541-0420.2005.00440.x
- M. J. Anderson and D. C. I. Walsh (2013). PERMANOVA, ANOSIM, and the Mantel test in the face of heterogeneous dispersions: what null hypothesis are you testing? *Ecological Monographs* 83(4), 557-574. doi:10.1890/12-2010.1
- A. C. Stier, K. M. Geange, K. M. Hanson and B. M. Bolker (2013). Predator density and timing of arrival affect reef fish community assembly. *Ecology* 94(5), 1057-1068. doi:10.1890/11-1983.1
- R. H. G. Jongman, C. J. F. ter Braak and O. F. R. van Tongeren (1987). *Data Analysis in Community and Landscape Ecology*. Pudoc, Wageningen.
- J. Oksanen et al. (2025). vegan: Community Ecology Package, R package version 2.7-3.
